    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.1.02-12",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Markets influence location through demand, product requirements, delivery time and the ability to reach customers. Some industries benefit from being near a large local market; others can produce near raw materials and use transport to reach distant buyers. A fish processor selling frozen products abroad needs suitable packaging, quality systems and shipping links, while a fresh local product may face tighter delivery constraints. The market is not simply a place on a map: it includes buyers with particular needs. A large nearby population does not automatically demand the exact product at a viable price.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-2.1.02-1",
        "CL-6137-2.1.02-2",
