    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.1.02-14",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Site factors concern the specific plot and its immediate environment. Space, slope, ground conditions, water, drainage, waste handling, expansion potential and proximity to sensitive uses can affect suitability. A low purchase price may be offset by preparation, flood exposure or inadequate utilities. A fish plant needs hygienic water and arrangements for wastewater and by-products; those requirements connect the site to the wider town and environment. A site should not be selected solely because it is empty. The question is whether it can support the intended activity without unacceptable constraints or effects.",
