    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.1.02-15",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Situation is the location relative to other places and networks, while site refers more directly to the plot’s own characteristics. A level plot with room to expand is a site advantage; closeness to a port or market is a situational relationship. The terms can interact in a practical decision, but separating them improves explanation. A map of routes provides evidence of situation, while a plan showing drainage and available land provides site evidence. Do not infer an unshown ground condition from a road connection or a market distance from a contour alone.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-2.1.02-1",
        "CL-6137-2.1.02-2",
