    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.1.02-18",
      "block_type": "worked_application",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Location factors can change in importance as technology, infrastructure, resources and markets change. Improved cold transport may extend the distance a plant can operate from landings, while a declining stock can undermine a previously favourable raw-material location. New services can improve an existing town’s attraction; congestion or environmental pressure can create constraints. A historical reason for location is not necessarily a complete explanation of current operation. Distinguish initial attraction, continuing advantages and later adaptation when the resource provides a time sequence.",
      "claim_ids": [
